5. FUNCTION-BY-FUNCTION BREAKDOWN
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.1 void setup()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Runs once at power-on. Initializes hardware and pre-generates 
the maximum possible sequence.

WHAT IT DOES:
  1. Sets buttonPin as INPUT_PULLUP
  2. Initializes the OLED display
  3. Clears the display buffer
  4. Seeds the random number generator using analogRead(0)
  5. Calls generateSequence() to fill the ENTIRE 50-element sequence 
     array with random 0s and 1s upfront (even though early rounds only 
     use the first 1-3 elements)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.2 void generateSequence()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Fills the sequence[] array with random binary values (0 or 1).

WHY GENERATE THE FULL 50-ELEMENT ARRAY UPFRONT?
  This design choice ensures the ENTIRE sequence for a full game session 
  is decided at the start, rather than generating new random values as 
  the player progresses. This means the sequence is fixed and consistent 
  once generated - important because in SHOWING_SEQUENCE state, the game 
  replays the FULL sequence from index 0 every round (not just the new 
  step), so the earlier steps must remain unchanged.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.3 void loop()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Main game state machine using a SWITCH statement (different from 
the if/else if structure used in Flappy Bird and Stop The Bar).

STRUCTURE:
  switch(state) {
    case START_SCREEN:       -> show title, wait for press to start
    case SHOWING_SEQUENCE:   -> call handleShowingSequence()
    case WAITING_INPUT:      -> call handleWaitingInput()
    case ROUND_SUCCESS:      -> show success message, then advance after 1000ms
    case ROUND_FAIL:         -> show failure message, then move to game over 
                                after 1500ms
    case GAME_OVER_STATE:    -> show final score, wait for press to restart
  }

NOTE: This switch-based state machine is a more formal/scalable pattern 
compared to the boolean-flag approach in earlier games, and is generally 
preferred when a game has more than 2-3 distinct states.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.4 void startNewGame()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Resets all game variables and begins a fresh game session.

WHAT IT DOES:
  1. sequenceLength = 1 (start with just 1 step to memorize)
  2. score = 0, level = 1
  3. showIndex = 0, inputIndex = 0
  4. generateSequence() called AGAIN (fresh random pattern each new game)
  5. state = SHOWING_SEQUENCE (begin showing the pattern immediately)
  6. stateTimer = millis() (start timing for the first flash)
  7. flashOn = false (ensures first flash starts fresh)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.5 void handleShowingSequence()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Manages the "watch the pattern" phase - displays each element of 
the sequence in order, with proper timing for flash-on, flash-off (gap), 
and advancing to the next element.

THIS IS A NON-BLOCKING TIMED ANIMATION using millis() - similar in 
concept to Flappy Bird's wing-flap timer, but more complex since it has 
THREE distinct timing phases per flash: ON, GAP, then ADVANCE.

LOGIC BREAKDOWN:

  A) DETERMINE CURRENT FLASH DURATION
     int currentDuration = sequence[showIndex] == 1 ? LONG_DURATION : SHORT_DURATION;
     Looks up whether the CURRENT step in the sequence is a 0 (short) or 
     1 (long), and selects the appropriate display duration (300ms or 700ms).

  B) IF FLASH HASN'T STARTED YET (flashOn == false):
     - Sets flashOn = true
     - Resets stateTimer = millis() (starts the clock for this flash)
     - Draws the flash immediately (drawFlash(..., true))

  C) IF FLASH IS ALREADY ON (flashOn == true), CHECK ELAPSED TIME:
     - IF elapsed < currentDuration:
         Still within the "ON" phase - keep redrawing the flash (drawFlash 
         with on=true). Note: redrawing every loop iteration during this 
         phase is slightly inefficient but harmless for OLED displays at 
         this scale.
     - ELSE IF elapsed < currentDuration + GAP_DURATION:
         Now in the "GAP" phase - draw blank/off screen (drawFlash with 
         on=false), creating a visible pause between flashes so 
         consecutive same-type flashes don't blur together visually.
     - ELSE (both ON and GAP phases have elapsed):
         Move to the NEXT element: showIndex++, reset flashOn = false, 
         reset stateTimer = millis()
         
         IF showIndex has reached sequenceLength (all elements shown):
           - inputIndex = 0 (prepare to receive player's first input)
           - state = WAITING_INPUT (switch to input-receiving state)
           - drawWaitingScreen() called immediately to show "YOUR TURN!"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.6 void handleWaitingInput()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Manages the "player reproduces the pattern" phase - detects 
button press/release cycles, measures hold duration, classifies each 
input as SHORT or LONG, and compares against the expected sequence.

LOGIC BREAKDOWN:

  A) READ CURRENT BUTTON STATE
     bool currentlyPressed = (digitalRead(buttonPin) == HIGH);

  B) DETECT PRESS START (button just went from not-pressed to pressed):
     if (currentlyPressed && !buttonWasPressed) {
       pressStartTime = millis();  // start timing this press
       buttonWasPressed = true;
     }

  C) DETECT RELEASE (button just went from pressed to not-pressed):
     if (!currentlyPressed && buttonWasPressed) {
       - Calculate pressDuration = millis() - pressStartTime
       - buttonWasPressed = false (reset for next press cycle)
       - Classify input: 
           playerInput = (pressDuration >= LONG_PRESS_THRESHOLD) ? 1 : 0
       - COMPARE against expected value: sequence[inputIndex]
       
       IF MATCH (correct input):
         - inputIndex++ (move to next expected step)
         - drawCorrectFeedback() shows brief "GOOD!" message (uses a 
           blocking delay(300) here - a deliberate exception to the 
           non-blocking pattern used elsewhere, acceptable since it's 
           very brief and provides important player feedback)
         - IF inputIndex has reached sequenceLength (all steps correctly 
           reproduced):
             state = ROUND_SUCCESS
             stateTimer = millis()
       
       IF MISMATCH (wrong input):
         - Update bestScore if current score is a new record
         - state = ROUND_FAIL
         - stateTimer = millis()

  D) LIVE VISUAL FEEDBACK WHILE BUTTON IS CURRENTLY HELD DOWN:
     if (currentlyPressed) {
       unsigned long heldTime = millis() - pressStartTime;
       drawHoldingFeedback(heldTime);
     }
     This provides REAL-TIME feedback showing a growing bar as the 
     player holds the button, with a threshold marker line - helping 
     players learn to calibrate their timing (this is a significant 
     UX improvement over a "blind" hold with no visual feedback).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.7 void drawFlash(int type, bool on)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Renders a single flash of the sequence during the watch phase.

PARAMETERS:
  - type: 0 (short) or 1 (long) - determines bar width and label
  - on: true (currently showing) or false (currently in gap/blank period)

WHAT IT DRAWS:
  1. Always shows HUD: current Level and Sequence length (top of screen)
  2. Always shows "WATCH CAREFULLY" instruction text
  3. IF on == true:
     - IF type == 1 (LONG): draws a WIDE rounded rectangle (100px wide) 
       with "LONG" label text in BLACK (inverted, since it's drawn on 
       top of the white filled bar)
     - IF type == 0 (SHORT): draws a NARROW rounded rectangle (40px wide) 
       with "SHORT" label text in BLACK
  4. IF on == false: the bar area remains blank (gap period), only HUD 
     and instruction text are shown

DESIGN NOTE: The visual size difference (wide vs narrow bar) reinforces 
the LONG/SHORT concept both through TEXT LABEL and VISUAL WIDTH 
simultaneously, aiding memorability.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.8 void drawWaitingScreen()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Displayed once when transitioning into WAITING_INPUT state, 
showing the player they should now begin their input.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - Level and Sequence length (HUD, same as during watch phase)
  - "YOUR TURN!" prompt
  - "Short tap / Long hold" reminder instruction
  - Current progress: "Step: [inputIndex+1]/[sequenceLength]" - shows 
    the player exactly which step of the sequence they're currently 
    expected to input

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.9 void drawHoldingFeedback(unsigned long heldTime)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Real-time visual feedback shown WHILE the button is currently 
being held down, updating continuously as long as the press continues.

WHAT IT DRAWS:
  1. "HOLDING..." status text
  2. A growing filled bar whose width is proportional to how long the 
     button has been held so far:
       barWidth = map(constrain(heldTime, 0, LONG_PRESS_THRESHOLD+200), 
                      0, LONG_PRESS_THRESHOLD+200, 0, 100)
     This maps the held duration (capped at threshold+200ms) to a 
     0-100 pixel width value.
  3. A vertical MARKER LINE positioned exactly at the LONG_PRESS_THRESHOLD 
     point within the bar - this shows the player EXACTLY where "short" 
     becomes "long", so they can visually see when they've crossed into 
     LONG territory while still holding the button.

DESIGN NOTE: This is a particularly clever piece of UX design for a 
memory/timing game - it turns an otherwise "blind" timing mechanic (the 
player can't normally see elapsed milliseconds) into a visually 
understandable action with clear real-time feedback.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.10 void drawCorrectFeedback()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Brief confirmation shown immediately after each correct input 
step (not just at the end of the full sequence).

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - Large text (size 2): "GOOD!"

NOTE: Uses delay(300) - a rare blocking call in this codebase, used 
deliberately here for simplicity since a brief 300ms freeze between 
input steps is imperceptible/acceptable in this context and greatly 
simplifies the feedback logic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.11 void drawRoundSuccess()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Shown when the ENTIRE sequence has been correctly reproduced, 
displayed for 1000ms (handled in the ROUND_SUCCESS case within loop()) 
before advancing to the next (longer) round.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "SEQUENCE COMPLETE!"
  - Current score
  - "Next round..." (indicates automatic progression, no button needed)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.12 void drawRoundFail()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Shown when the player makes an incorrect input, displayed for 
1500ms before transitioning to the Game Over screen.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "WRONG!"
  - "Expected: LONG" or "Expected: SHORT" - tells the player exactly 
    what input was expected at the point they failed (educational 
    feedback, helps players understand their mistake)
  - Current score (final score, since the game is about to end)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.13 void drawGameOver()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Final results screen shown after ROUND_FAIL transitions here.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "GAME OVER"
  - "Final Score: [score]"
  - "Best Score: [bestScore]"
  - "Press to Play Again"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5.14 void drawStartScreen()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PURPOSE: Title/instructions screen shown before gameplay begins.

CONTENT SHOWN:
  - "MEMORY BEEP" title
  - "Watch the pattern," / "then repeat it back!" instructions
  - "Short tap / Long hold" control reminder
  - "Press to Start" prompt

