7. KEY TECHNICAL CONCEPTS DEMONSTRATED
================================================================================

1. ENUM-BASED STATE MACHINE
   Unlike the simpler boolean-flag approach in Flappy Bird and Stop The 
   Bar, this game uses a proper C++ enum with a switch statement - a 
   more scalable and readable pattern when managing 6+ distinct game 
   states.

2. ENCODING COMPLEX INPUT WITH A SINGLE BUTTON
   Since traditional Simon Says requires 4 buttons/colors, this game 
   demonstrates a clever workaround: encoding a binary sequence (0/1) 
   using PRESS DURATION instead of press location/color. This is a key 
   lesson in adapting classic game concepts to constrained hardware.

3. MULTI-PHASE NON-BLOCKING TIMED ANIMATION
   handleShowingSequence() manages THREE sequential timing phases 
   (ON -> GAP -> ADVANCE) per sequence element, all using millis() 
   comparisons rather than delay(), allowing the button to remain 
   responsive even during "animation" playback (though input isn't 
   accepted until WAITING_INPUT state).

4. PRESS-AND-RELEASE DURATION MEASUREMENT
   Building on simpler edge detection (seen in Stop The Bar), this game 
   goes further by measuring the FULL press-to-release duration to 
   classify input into two categories (short/long), rather than just 
   detecting a single press event.

5. REAL-TIME VISUAL FEEDBACK DURING INPUT
   drawHoldingFeedback() continuously updates WHILE the button is held, 
   giving the player live feedback about their timing relative to the 
   threshold - a UX pattern not present in the simpler games, valuable 
   for any timing-sensitive input mechanic.

6. PRE-GENERATED VS. ON-DEMAND RANDOM SEQUENCES
   Unlike Flappy Bird (which generates new pipe positions on-demand as 
   old ones scroll off-screen), this game pre-generates the ENTIRE 
   sequence upfront and replays it from the beginning each round - a 
   necessary approach for "remember the growing pattern" style games.

7. EDUCATIONAL FAILURE FEEDBACK
   drawRoundFail() explicitly tells the player what was EXPECTED at the 
   point of failure, rather than just saying "wrong" - a thoughtful 
   design choice that helps players understand and improve.

